  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nToday through Saturday...Increased troughing across the eastern half \nof the United States, has forced a frontal boundary south and \nthrough the forecast area. In the boundary's wake, slightly drier \nair has begun filtering south into the region.  This drier air, \nalong with some cloud cover today across the north, will provide \nsome temporary relief from the oppressive heat that's gripped the \nregion for some time. As dew points both today and Monday mix into \nthe mid 60s and lower 70s, highs today will range from the middle \n80s to lower 90s and from the upper 80s to middle 90s come Monday.\n\nUnfortunately, this'll all be short-lived as we move into the mid \nand latter portions of the forecast period.  Strong high pressure \naloft will re-assert itself across the Lower Mississippi River \nValley during this time.  High temperatures will again begin \nclimbing back into the mid and upper 90s areawide. Moisture levels \nwill also begin creeping up mid and late week.  This currently looks \nto again lead to a bout of dangerous heat stress conditions across \nmost, if not all, of the CWA as we end the forecast period. Rain \nchances will also be tough to come by during this time, with only an \nisolated shower or storm possible during the heat of the day. /19/",
